#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bin_counts

#% text
# `bin_counts(xs, w)` counts the real numbers of `xs` in the bins `[k w, (k + 1) w)`, from the bin of the smallest to that of the largest. `bin_counts(xs, [a, b, dx])` counts in the bins `[a, a + dx)`, `[a + dx, a + 2dx)`, ... up to `b`, and `bin_counts(xs, [[e1, e2, ...]])` in the bins between consecutive edges (which may be `-inf` and `inf`). Elements that are not real numbers are left out.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], 2)
#% output
#= [2, 3, 2, 1]

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [0, 10, 1])
#% output
#= [0, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0]

#% cell
bin_counts([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [[-inf, 2, 5, 7, inf]])
#% output
#= [2, 4, 2, 0]

#% cell
bin_counts([1.5, 3, a, 2.5, 1, im], 2)
#% output
#= [2, 2]

